! mcp_bootstrap_p187.strap -- MCP printed page 187: the EOP (end of
! operation) test for SEOP-type I/O, the SEOP wait loop, the "only
! CS is on" connective test, and the one-word status work area K WS
! that every row on the page reads bits out of.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.194.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 400 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID LF000001   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  187".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK. <<<
! Blank paper after the word LOCATION at the right-hand end of the
! heading line, with the dashed rule running through it -- NO
! DIGITS, and none invented.  That is the case the workflow
! predicts: the field is LINE 1'S location, line 1 here is an
! asterisk banner that compiles nothing, so the field is empty.  A
! blank does NOT mean the page has no origin; this page's first
! LOCATED row is line 6 at 221350.40.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-ONE PRINTED LINES on TWENTY-SIX CARDS.  Lines 1-5 are a
! banner and a calling-sequence comment; lines 6-29 and 31 compile;
! line 30 is a CNOP that emits nothing (see LISTING MARKS).  No
! SLC, no SYN, no VF, no DD.  ONE reservation, line 31's one-word
! DRZ, and it is the page's last row.
!
! The page is CONTIGUOUS from 221350.40 to 221367.77 -- fifteen and
! a half words of code plus the one reserved word -- with NO HOLE,
! and then blank paper to the foot of the form.
!
!   lines  6-10   K SEOP T, the SEOP wait loop: load the retry
!                 count, copy the channel's control word into
!                 K WS, branch-on-zero-EKJ back round, count down
!                 and give up at K REJT.
!   line  11      load six status bits out of K WS.
!   lines 12-16   K EOP R, the indicator test: branch if none on,
!                 loop if the SEOP bit is on, the CI0001 connective
!                 that asks whether ONLY CS is on, and the
!                 branch-on-zero-bit to Z TSEOP.
!   lines 17-25   the save/restore arm -- SVA the return into
!                 R SVRTN, clear the control word, reload XR1, two
!                 KVI/BXE dispatch pairs, and R SVRTN itself.
!   lines 26-29   Z TSEOP, the "true SEOP" arm: count-branch-zero
!                 to K SOS, clear the control word, retry.
!   line  30      CNOP -- prints NOTHING, see below.
!   line  31      K WS, the one-word status work area.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SPAN, ESTABLISHED FROM THE PAGE (no survey endpoint used)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! I was told not to quote survey endpoints and I did not read them.
! The span comes from three columns of this page:
!
!   * the LINE column runs 1..31 with no gap, 31 is the last line
!     printed and there is blank paper below it;
!   * the CARD SERIAL column runs LF000001..LF000026 with no gap,
!     and the heading's own "CARD ID LF000001" agrees with line 1,
!     so nothing fell off either end;
!   * the counter chain closes digit for digit from 221350.40 to
!     221367.00 with every intermediate location printed.
!
!   FIRST LOCATED ROW   line  6   221350.40
!   LAST LOCATED ROW    line 31   221367.00  (a one-word DRZ)
!   ROWS                31 printed lines, 26 cards, 24 !CHECKs
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! CARD SERIALS: FIVE PRINT TWICE, AND THEY CONFIRM p.184's RULE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! 31 lines, 26 serials.  Five serials carry two listing lines:
!
!   lines  9/10  LF000009   CB,1,$-1.32   ;  B,K REJT
!   lines 18/19  LF000017   SIC,KCBJR     ;  B,KCLEAR
!   lines 21/22  LF000019   KVI,$1,ZLOCXX ;  BXE,($1)
!   lines 23/24  LF000020   KVI,$1,KKMODE ;  BXE,($1)
!   lines 27/28  LF000023   SIC,K CBJ R   ;  B,K CLEAR
!
! printed p.184 found five such pairs and concluded the rule is
! "ONE CARD, ONE WORD" and not "one card, one SIC pair", because
! one of its five was an LVI/SVA pair.  THIS PAGE CONFIRMS IT ON A
! SECOND PAGE and strengthens it: only two of my five are SIC
! pairs, two are KVI/BXE and one is CB/B.  Every pair is two
! HALF-WORD instructions totalling one word.
!
! The page shows the convention in its own prose.  Line 4's comment
! card reads
!     CALLING SEQUENCE  **  SIC,K EOP R?B,K SEOP T
! where the character printed between "R" and "B" is NOT a plus
! sign: at 1600 dpi it is a vertical bar crossed by TWO horizontals.
! >>> I DESCRIBE THE SHAPE AND I DO NOT NAME IT. <<<  strap/
! charsets.py has no such character and neither volume's OCR
! contains the words "record mark", so a name would be recall and
! not a citation -- brief rule 1.  What IS evidence is the SHAPE
! plus the USAGE: it stands between two half-word instructions, and
! printed p.186 line 12's comment puts the same construction (there
! written "SIC,K UNR R ** B,K UNR", per p.184's deck) in the same
! place.  Nothing on this page depends on it: line 4 compiles
! nothing, and the LISTING prints the two instructions of a
! two-instruction card as two lines with two locations, which is
! what the deck does too.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN, BOTH WAYS -- AND BOTH ENDS ARE WITNESSED OFF OTHER
! PAGES' INK, NOT MINE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! BACKWARD.  This page prints NO SLC card, so the deck's
! "SLC,(8)221350.40" is a substitution for a counter that simply
! arrived.  But it is not an assertion:
!
!   * THE LAST ROW OF printed p.186 THAT THE VOLUME OCR READS
!     (OCR of PDF p.193, a DIFFERENT page) is its line 51,
!         221350.00   221350.10 00   K CONV R   B,$
!     a HALF word at 221350.00, so the counter leaves p.186 at
!     221350.40, which is exactly this page's line 6.  And K CONV R
!     is not a guess either: three other pages print
!     "SIC,K CONV R" compiling 221350.00 80, and a SIC carries its
!     operand's full 24-bit bit address, so that word is pinned to
!     the bit.
!     >>> SAID CAREFULLY, BECAUSE AN OCR ENDPOINT IS A FLOOR. <<<
!     The workflow measured that mcp_ocr gives up mid-page (printed
!     p.181 has 56 lines and the reader quits at 42), so "line 51"
!     bounds what the READER read, not what p.186 PRINTS.  If p.186
!     prints rows below 51, the OCR lost them and the forward join
!     is p.186's to state.  Nothing here depends on it either way:
!     my origin is checked by the merge (next item), not by p.186.
!
!   * >>> AND THE ORIGIN IS CHECKED, NOT ASSERTED.  MEASURED. <<<
!     Five landed decks SYN symbols THIS page defines --
!     pp.152, 154, 159, 164 and 165 all carry
!         KSEOPT  SYN, (8)221350.40
!         KEOPR   SYN, (8)221354.40
!     and several of those references are SICs, hence bit-exact.
!     Injection run against the merged body image with this page
!     added, moving nothing but the SLC:
!
!         origin as printed  (8)221350.40  -> merge OK
!                                             771 defined, 66 external
!         origin shifted      (8)221351.00 -> REFUSED
!            FAIL KEOPR: printed p.152 SYNs bit 4766496,
!                        printed p.187 places it at bit 4766528
!
!     (The merge was run over pp.99-184 plus this page; pp.185 and
!     186 are not landed yet, so the run is the body image as it
!     stands today with p.187 appended.)
!
!     So this is the "CHECKED" case of the workflow's item 2, not
!     the "asserted" one.
!
! FORWARD.  Line 31 is a ONE-WORD DRZ at 221367.00, so
! >>> THE COUNTER LEAVES THIS PAGE AT 221370.00. <<<
! The volume OCR of printed p.188 (PDF p.195, a DIFFERENT page)
! prints "221370.00" against its line 4 SLC and against its line 8
! "K IO IND L(BU,2),S IO IND($13)".  A printed SLC is the best
! forward-join witness there is, and p.188 supplies one.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> FALSIFIABLE PREDICTIONS FOR printed p.188 (PDF p.195) <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   (1) its first LOCATED row is 221370.00, and it is a printed
!       "SLC,$" -- which, if it is really an SLC, is a free
!       confirmation of this page's forward join.  If p.188's
!       first location is anything but 221370.00, one of us is
!       wrong and the one-word DRZ on my line 31 is the first
!       thing to re-read.
!   (2) its first card serial is LF000027.  WEAKER THAN (1): the
!       brief records two measured cases of a serial that prints
!       NO listing line (ED000049, IH000041), both at page breaks,
!       which is exactly here.  A missing LF000027 would not
!       falsify the join; a serial BELOW LF000027 would.
!   (3) its heading-row LOCATION field is BLANK, because the
!       volume OCR shows p.188's lines 1-3 are an asterisk banner
!       ("****** I/O INDICATOR CHECK ******") and its first
!       located row is line 4.
!   (4) p.188 DEFINES none of the five names this page defines
!       (KSEOPT, KEOPR, RSVRTN, ZTSEOP, KWS).  It does define
!       K IO IND and K K IND.
!
! AND ONE FOR printed p.186's TRANSCRIBER:
!   (5) p.186's last located row is 221350.00 and is a HALF word
!       ("K CONV R  B,$"), so its page leaves the counter at
!       221350.40.  If p.186's ink says otherwise, say so loudly:
!       my line 6 depends on it, though my ORIGIN does not (see
!       the injection above).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT HAD TO BE SUBSTITUTED, AND IT IS LOUD
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> TWO ROWS ARE NOT WRITTEN AS THE PAGE PRINTS THEM. <<<
! Lines 11 and 13 print a NAME WITH A BLANK IN IT FOLLOWED BY A
! BIT OFFSET:
!
!     line 11   L(BU,6),K WS.19
!     line 13   BB,K WS.24,K SEOP T
!
! and strap_asm REFUSES both -- "undefined symbol 'K'" -- which is
! the brief's known gap ("a blank inside a name INSIDE AN
! EXPRESSION is refused") showing up on a third shape: name plus
! BIT OFFSET, where the two cases already recorded were name plus
! a word term (S TLR+14., K GOOF+.32).  The deck writes KWS.19 and
! KWS.24 CLOSED UP.  Measured, so the gap is bounded rather than
! guessed:
!
!     L(BU,6),K WS.19        -> REFUSED  undefined symbol 'K'
!     L(BU,6),KWS.19         -> assembles, prints the page's own
!                               221367.23 80 006000.20 50
!     CCW,0.0($14),K WS      -> ASSEMBLES.  A blanked name as a
!                               BARE operand is fine; it is only
!                               the ".NN" that breaks it.
!
! So line 7 keeps its printed blank and lines 11 and 13 do not.
! THE PRINTED FORMS ARE "K WS.19" AND "K WS.24"; the page itself
! writes the same symbol both ways (line 16 prints "KWS.21" closed
! up and line 31's NAME field prints "KWS"), so the substitution
! is to a spelling this page also uses -- but it is still a
! substitution and it is recorded here because a later reader
! cannot see it in the deck.
!
! The five NAMES this page defines are written closed up for the
! same reason p.184 gives: strap_asm cannot carry a blank in a
! LABEL field at all.  Printed: "K SEOP T", "K EOP R", "RSVRTN",
! "ZTSEOP", "KWS".  Three of the five print closed up already.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) >>> THE PAGE'S ROUND GLYPH IS SETTLED BY THE ASSEMBLER, NOT
!     BY SHAPE.  Line 14's immediate reads "(.5,2)1111C1" to the
!     eye at 1600 dpi.  Handed to strap_asm, BOTH readings:
!
!       CI0001(BU,6),(.5,2)1111C1  -> "undefined symbol 'C1'"
!       CI0001(BU,6),(.5,2)111101  -> 750000.00 80 406000.02 70,
!                                     the page's own printed word
!
!     A radix-2 literal cannot hold a C, so the glyph is a 0 --
!     ladder rung 3 used as the eliminator it is, and then rung 5
!     and the compiled word agreeing with it.  0o75 = 111101 is
!     the six-bit immediate left-justified in A24, which is a
!     third, independent statement of the same six characters.
!
! (2) "EOP", banner line 2 and the names K EOP R / KEOPR.  The
!     banner prints "ECP TEST FCR SEOP TYPE I/C" to the eye.  It
!     is settled by ENGLISH IN THE SAME ROW, which is rung 1:
!     "FOR" is a word and prints with the SAME open round glyph as
!     the second character of "ECP", while "SEOP" four words later
!     prints a CLOSED one off the same drum in the same line.  So
!     the row contains both an open O and a closed O, the open one
!     inside a known English word, and the reading is
!     "EOP TEST FOR SEOP TYPE I/O".  EOP is end-of-operation and
!     SEOP is select-end-of-operation, which is what the page
!     tests.  NOTE WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is not a width or
!     openness argument (p.184 measured that those separate
!     nothing on this drum); it is one known English word next to
!     one unknown one.
!
! (3) K CBJ R (lines 18, 27).  Settled before this page: p.184's
!     GLYPH CALL (8) corrected "K OBJ R" to "K CBJ R" off p.186's
!     defining card and off the typeset index's ALPHABETICAL
!     POSITION in C22-6728.  Both my rows compile 221313.40 80,
!     the same half word p.184 line 24 prints, and line 27 prints
!     the name with its blanks in while line 18 prints it closed
!     up -- one page, one symbol, two spellings.
!
! (4) DRZ, line 31.  The Z IS READ OFF THE INK at 1600 dpi and
!     not off a passing run: the workflow records that DR and DRZ
!     print the identical BINARY OUTPUT (the reservation's COUNT),
!     so no !CHECK on this page can tell them apart.  At 1600 dpi
!     the three characters D R Z are unambiguous -- the D has the
!     straight vertical left stem and squared corners that
!     separate it from C and 0, and the Z is a Z.  >>> FLAGGED AS
!     INK-ONLY.  If this is wrong, the merged image gains one word
!     of unspecified content at 221367 where it should have a word
!     of zeros, and nothing in this repository would notice. <<<
!
! (5) The mode letter in "DRZ(N),(1)" is N, read at 1600 dpi.
!     strap_asm accepts mode N and the row compiles the printed
!     000001.00, but a reservation prints its COUNT, so THE MODE
!     IS UNCHECKED BY ANYTHING.  It is ink only, like the Z.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LISTING MARKS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every line number carries the ordinary trailing asterisk.
! THREE ROWS CARRY A SECOND ASTERISK AFTER THE LOCATION:
!
!     line  8   221352.00 *   BZEKJZ,$+1.32
!     line 27   221365.40 *   SIC,K CBJ R
!     line 31   221367.00 *   KWS  DRZ(N),(1)
!
! Recorded, NO RULE PROPOSED -- the brief is explicit that a rule
! from one page gets refuted the same afternoon.  What this page
! does do is kill two candidate rules that its three marks might
! otherwise suggest, and it kills them with its OWN rows:
!
!   * NOT "every SIC".  Line 27 is a marked SIC and line 18 is an
!     UNMARKED SIC compiling the identical half word from the
!     identical statement.  Two SIC rows, one page, one marked.
!   * NOT "every label row".  Line 31 is marked and carries the
!     name KWS; lines 6, 12, 25 and 26 carry names and are not.
!   * NOT "every branch".  Line 8 is marked; the page's eleven
!     other branches are not.
!
! So the three have nothing in common on this page either: one
! branch, one SIC, one reservation; one of the three is a label
! row and two are not.  Add them to the list of things the
! asterisk is not and move on.
!
! >>> AND LINE 30's CNOP PRINTS NO LOCATION AND NO BINARY. <<<
! Worth one line because it is the exact complement of printed
! p.184 line 31, which prints "221236.40  000000.30 00  CNOP".
! There the counter arrived at an ODD half word and the CNOP
! emitted one; here line 29 ends at 221367.00, already word
! aligned, so the CNOP emits nothing and the listing prints an
! empty row.  Two pages, the same pseudo-op, both behaviours, and
! strap_asm reproduces both.  This row therefore writes NO !CHECK
! -- there is nothing to check -- and it still costs a card
! (LF000025).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- EIGHT, AND SIX ARE ALREADY LANDED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every one is written in the form the landed deck uses, so the
! merged image cannot disagree about a spelling or a value:
!
!   K REP     30          p.125 "KREP SYN, 30" -- A COUNT, not an
!                         address.  The defining card elsewhere in
!                         the volume prints "K REP SYN(BU,18),30";
!                         this deck copies p.125's dds-less form
!                         because an LCI takes an immediate count
!                         and nothing here inherits a length.
!   K REJT    221300.0    p.125; line 10 B, half word only
!   K SOS     221274.0    p.125; line 26 CBZ, half word only
!   K CBJ R   221313.40   pp.125/126/184; lines 18 and 27 are
!                         SICs, so BIT-EXACT from this page
!   K CLEAR   221302.40   pp.125/126/184; lines 19 and 28 B
!   K K MODE  217624.40   pp.151/152/154/183; line 23 KVI
!   Z LOCXX   220046.0    DEFINED by printed p.159 line 39,
!                         "ZLOCXX CM0000(BU,1),SSEL($11)".  My
!                         reference is a KVI, an INDEX-class
!                         operation, so this page sees the half
!                         word only; the value and the dds both
!                         come from p.159 and the merge checks
!                         them.  SYNned with NO dds on purpose --
!                         p.159's defining card carries it.
!   K RETRY   221263.0    >>> THE ONE EXTERNAL WHOSE DEFINING PAGE
!                         IS NOT LANDED. <<<  The volume OCR of
!                         printed p.185 line 12 prints
!                         "221263.00  000004.00 10  K RETRY
!                         LX,0,S IO INS($12)", and p.186 line 9's
!                         "K CBJ R  B,K RETRY" compiles 221263.10
!                         exactly as my line 29 does.  p.185 is
!                         being transcribed in this same block, so
!                         if the merge refuses K RETRY, p.185's
!                         deck is where to look, not this one.
!
! WHICH OF THEM THIS PAGE READS TO THE BIT, AND WHICH IT DOES NOT:
!   BIT-EXACT   K CBJ R (lines 18, 27 are SICs).
!   HALF WORD   K REJT, K SOS, K CLEAR, K K MODE, Z LOCXX --
!               branches and index operations, which spend their
!               low bits on their own operation field.  Where this
!               deck writes ".0" the bit is this corpus's
!               convention and NOT a reading; the values come from
!               the landed decks and from the OCR'd defining
!               cards, not from my page's binary.
!   NOT AN ADDRESS AT ALL   K REP, a count of 30.
!
! >>> AND LINE 7's CCW DOES NOT READ K WS's BIT OFFSET EITHER,
! >>> ALTHOUGH ITS SECOND WORD LOOKS LIKE AN ADDRESS. <<<
! It prints "221367.21 00".  The 0o21 is NOT a bit offset: the
! half word's octal pair is instruction bits 50-55, and an I-O
! instruction spends 51-54 on the OP field (CCW = 0b1000) followed
! by the literal 10000 of the pocket card's 9-bit code, so bits
! 50-55 read 0,1000,1 = 0o21 whatever K WS's offset is.  The
! address part is the word 221367 and nothing below it.  K WS is
! at 221367.00 because THIS PAGE'S LINE 31 PRINTS IT THERE, not
! because line 7 says so.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (A) >>> K SOS: THE ROUND GLYPH IS OPEN AND I DID NOT CLOSE IT.
!     Line 26 prints "CBZ,$11,KSOS", four characters K S ? S, and
!     the third is a round glyph.  Every rung of the ladder came
!     back empty or self-referential:
!       * rung 1, the comment column: line 26 HAS NO COMMENT.
!       * rung 2, the typeset manuals: no KS entry I can reach
!         from this page; p.184 showed the C22-6728 index is
!         decisive when the name is in it, and this one is the
!         case where it is not.
!       * rung 3, the assembler: the name is an operand, so both
!         readings assemble.  Silent, as the workflow warns.
!       * rungs 4 and 5 do not apply: the statement is printed
!         ONCE on this page and there is no octal field in it.
!       * the volume OCR SPLITS -- "K SOS" at its line 9549
!         (p.185's defining card, "221274.00 * 003405.35 00
!         K SOS LVI,14,S IO ERR") and "K SCS" at 9705 and 9564 --
!         and that is the same drum read twice, not a vote.  The
!         workflow says so in as many words.
!     WHAT IT RESTS ON: printed p.125's landed deck writes
!     "KSOS SYN, (8)221274.0" and this deck agrees with it, so the
!     merge will pair them whichever way the glyph goes.  The
!     ADDRESS is not in doubt; only the one character is.
!     WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: printed p.185's transcriber rendering
!     its line 21 NAME field at 1600 dpi beside a known digit 0 or
!     a known letter O in the SAME ROW -- its statement
!     "LVI,14,S IO ERR" carries an "IO" whose O is a known letter,
!     one column away.  That is the measurement I could not make
!     from here, because p.185's ink is not my page.
!
! (B) THE BIT OFFSETS OF THE FIVE HALF-WORD EXTERNALS, which is
!     the workflow's item 1 and is not news -- but the page gave
!     me no SIC and no VFL operand for any of them, so I am
!     explicitly NOT claiming to have read them.  The one VFL row
!     that could have read an external, line 11's L(BU,6), reads
!     K WS, which this page DEFINES; it prints 221367.23 against
!     line 31's printed LOCATION of 221367.00 plus the decimal
!     offset .19 = 0o23, which is a real check of the conversion
!     and not of an external.
!
! (C) THE RECORD-MARK GLYPH in line 4's comment (see CARD SERIALS).
!     I can describe the shape and I can show that the listing
!     uses it where two instructions share a card, but I cannot
!     cite a manual for STRAP's own spelling of it, and line 4
!     compiles nothing, so nothing here forces the question.
!
! (D) THE MODE LETTER N and the Z of DRZ on line 31 (GLYPH CALLS
!     4 and 5).  Both are ink-only and no check on this page or in
!     the merged image can see either.
!
! (E) The brief's open question -- does a mode-only dds "(BU)"
!     inherit a LENGTH from its operand symbol? -- IS NOT TOUCHED
!     BY THIS PAGE.  Every dds here writes an explicit length
!     ((BU,6) twice, (BU,2) none, (N) on a reservation), so there
